## Session-End Logic

A "session" is the period during which the player is active. Ending the session may trigger a confirmation modal.

### What ends a session?

1. **User clicks Close (X) on the player** → check for watched videos:
   - If watched videos exist → show **Session-end confirmation modal**
   - If no watched videos → close player immediately, no modal
2. **Last unwatched video finishes playing (queue runs out)** → show **Queue-complete confirmation modal** (always shown, even if no watched videos? See note below)
3. **User clicks Clear All** in modal or panel → check for watched videos:
   - If watched videos exist → show **Session-end confirmation modal**, with the action being clear-all rather than just close
   - If no watched videos → clear immediately with **undo toast** (see below)
4. **User closes the browser tab or navigates away** → no modal possible; state persists as-is. User returns later to same state.

**Note on case 2:** "Queue complete" means the queue ran out of unwatched videos. If the user has been watching, there will be watched videos to archive. If somehow the queue ran out without any watched videos (edge case — user manually removed everything while playing?), the player just closes silently. The Queue-complete modal only appears when there is something to archive.

### Confirmation modal — two variants

Both modals have the same button options:
- **Archive Videos** (confirms): all videos with `watched: true` are added to the archive system (using the same archive flow that the existing X-on-thumbnail uses). They are then removed from the queue. Unwatched videos remain in the queue. Player closes.
- **Return to Queue** (cancels): modal closes, player stays active, no state changes.

**Variant A — Session-end (triggered by closing player or Clear All):**

> **End session?**
> 
> Watched videos will be archived.
> 
> *Once the dashboard is refreshed, archived videos will disappear. You won't be able to access them.*
> 
> [Archive Videos]  [Return to Queue]

**Variant B — Queue complete (triggered when last unwatched video finishes):**

> **Queue complete**
> 
> You've reached the end of your queue. Watched videos will be archived.
> 
> *Once the dashboard is refreshed, archived videos will disappear. You won't be able to access them.*
> 
> [Archive Videos]  [Return to Queue]

The user (Kate) may revise the exact wording later. The important thing is the LOGIC for selecting which modal appears.

### Clear All — undo toast (no-stakes variant)

When the user clicks Clear All and there are NO watched videos in the queue (e.g., they're still building the queue and haven't played anything yet), no confirmation modal is needed. Instead:

- Queue is cleared immediately.
- A toast/snackbar appears at the bottom of the screen with text like "Queue cleared" and an "Undo" link/button.
- Toast stays visible for **15 seconds**, then fades out.
- Clicking "Undo" restores the queue to its pre-clear state.
- After fade-out or any other queue modification, undo is no longer available.

This pattern only applies when there are no watched videos. If watched videos exist, the full confirmation modal is used instead (because the stakes are higher — losing archive logging).

